"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[83485],{183485:e=>{e.exports='# Spark Connect Compatibility Skill\n\nThis skill ensures generated code is compatible with Spark Connect, the client/server execution mode used on Standard and Serverless compute.\n\n## Architecture\n\nSpark Connect decouples the Python process from the JVM via a protobuf-over-gRPC protocol. The user\'s Python code runs in a thin client process, while the Spark driver and executors run on the server. This means:\n\n- **No direct JVM access** from Python: `_jvm`, `_jdf`, `_jcol`, `_jsc` are unavailable.\n- **RDD is a driver-local concept**: RDD APIs require a co-located driver and are not serializable over gRPC.\n- **SparkContext is server-side only**: `spark.sparkContext`, `sc`, and related APIs are not exposed to the client.\n\n## Compute Modes\n\n| Mode                   | Execution Model      | SC-Compatible Code Required |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| **Serverless**         | Spark Connect        | Yes                         |\n| **Standard (Shared)**  | Spark Connect        | Yes                         |\n| **Assigned / Classic** | Direct JVM (in-proc) | No (but recommended)        |\n\n## Unsupported API Categories\n\n| Category           | Examples                                     | Alternative                                                  |\n| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| RDD API            | `spark.sparkContext.parallelize()`, `df.rdd` | DataFrame / SQL API                                          |\n| SparkContext       | `sc`, `spark.sparkContext`                   | `spark` (SparkSession) only                                  |\n| JVM private access | `spark._jvm`, `df._jdf`, `col._jcol`         | Public DataFrame/Column API                                  |\n| queryExecution     | `df.queryExecution`                          | `df.explain()`, `df.schema`                                  |\n| experimental       | `spark.experimental`                         | Built-in DataFrame methods                                   |\n| sessionState       | `spark.sessionState`                         | `spark.conf`, SQL (`USE`/`SHOW`/`DESCRIBE`)                  |\n| DBFS / FUSE mounts | `/dbfs/mnt/...`, `dbutils.fs` (some methods) | Unity Catalog Volumes                                        |\n| File path metadata | `input_file_name()`                          | `col("_metadata.file_name")` or `col("_metadata.file_path")` |\n\n## Connect vs Classic Behavioral Differences\n\n**Spark Connect uses lazy schema analysis** — unlike Classic Spark where schema analysis is eager. Key consequences:\n\n- **Column reference errors are deferred**: A typo like `col("nonexistent_col")` does not raise an error when the transformation is defined. The error only surfaces when an action (`collect()`, `show()`, `count()`, `write`) or explicit schema access (`df.columns`, `df.schema`, `df.printSchema()`) is called. When explaining this behavior, always call it "Spark Connect lazy schema analysis" and suggest `df.columns`, `df.schema`, or `df.printSchema()` after transformations to catch errors early.\n\n- **Overwriting temp views causes stale DataFrame references**: Previously-created DataFrame variables resolve to the **current** view definition at action time, not the one at creation time. This is expected Spark Connect behavior (lazy plan analysis), but differs from Classic Spark and is a common source of surprise. Always warn users about this when they overwrite temp views in loops or pipelines. See `skills/spark-connect/knowledge/api-anti-patterns.md` for full detail and fixes.\n\n- **MLflow Spark integrations use unavailable JVM APIs**: `mlflow.spark.autolog()` and `mlflow.spark.log_model()` internally call SparkContext and JVM APIs that are not available on Spark Connect. They are **not supported** on Standard or Serverless compute. Alternatives that work: `mlflow.log_param()`, `mlflow.log_metric()`, `mlflow.sklearn.log_model()`, `mlflow.pyfunc.log_model()`.\n\n- **Hive JAR UDFs are blocked**: `CREATE FUNCTION ... USING JAR` (Hive UDFs loaded from JARs) are blocked because they allow arbitrary JVM classloader access, breaking the cluster isolation model required by Standard/Serverless. Supported alternatives: (1) session-based Scala UDFs via `spark.udf.register()` in a Scala notebook cell (DBR 14.2+), or (2) Unity Catalog Python UDFs via `CREATE FUNCTION ... LANGUAGE PYTHON`.\n\nSee the anti-pattern sections in `skills/spark-connect/knowledge/api-anti-patterns.md` for detailed guidance on temp views, UDF closures, error handling, and Analyze RPC overhead.\n\n## Behavioral Rules\n\n1. **NEVER generate SC-incompatible code** when the user is on Standard or Serverless compute.\n2. **Prefer DataFrame API over RDD API** in all code suggestions, regardless of compute mode.\n3. When user code uses an incompatible pattern, suggest the SC-compatible alternative immediately.\n4. Reference the knowledge files below for specific pattern-to-fix mappings.\n5. When unsure whether an API is SC-compatible, err on the side of using the DataFrame API.\n6. When you need to verify whether a specific PySpark API is supported in Spark Connect or check version-specific behavior, use the Spark API tools (spark_search_apis, spark_get_api_info) if available in your tool list.\n7. **NEVER use `input_file_name()`** when the user needs the source file name/path — use `col("_metadata.file_name")` or `col("_metadata.file_path")` instead. `input_file_name()` is blocked by Unity Catalog on Standard and Serverless compute. Example: when a user asks to "read a CSV file and add a column with the source file name", always generate:\n   ```python\n   df = spark.read.csv("/path/to/data")\n   df = df.withColumn("source_file", col("_metadata.file_name"))\n   ```\n   **Never** generate `df.withColumn("source_file", input_file_name())`.\n\n## Sub-Topics\n\n**Immediately load the matching knowledge file before generating any code or fix.** Do not answer until the relevant file is loaded:\n\n| File                                                    | When to Load                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `skills/spark-connect/knowledge/api-surface.md`         | **MUST load** if user encounters: RDD API usage (`df.rdd`, `.rdd.map()`, `sc.parallelize`), `JVM_ATTRIBUTE_NOT_SUPPORTED` error, SparkContext access (`sc`, `spark.sparkContext`), JVM accessors (`_jvm`, `_jdf`, `_jcol`), catalog API issues, DBFS paths, job tagging / job description / Spark UI labels (`addTag`, `setJobDescription`), log levels (`setLogLevel`), deeply nested DataFrames / protobuf recursion limit                                             |\n| `skills/spark-connect/knowledge/api-anti-patterns.md`   | User hits subtle behavioral differences that produce silently wrong results or performance issues: overwriting temp views in loops / stale DataFrame references / `createOrReplaceTempView` reuse, UDF closures over mutable variables / late-binding UDF bugs, column reference errors deferred to action time / lazy schema analysis / why errors only appear at `collect()`/`show()`/`count()`, excessive `df.columns`/`df.schema` in loops / Analyze RPC performance |\n| `skills/spark-connect/knowledge/common-issues.md`       | **MUST load** if user reports: Pandas UDF row count mismatch, `Output row count does not match input row count` error, row-by-row yield inside Iterator UDF, `display()` errors on image DataFrames, `mlflow.spark.autolog()` / `mlflow.spark.log_model()` failures, or MLflow autologging compatibility issues on Standard or Serverless clusters                                                                                                                       |\n| `skills/spark-connect/knowledge/feature-limitations.md` | User asks about `input_file_name`, reading files and adding source file name or path column, Hive UDFs, JAR UDFs, `CREATE FUNCTION ... USING JAR`, registering UDFs from JAR files on shared clusters, `registerJavaFunction`, instance profiles, JDBC, MLlib, GraphFrames, or RDD API on Standard/Serverless compute                                                                                                                                                    |\n'}}]);
